WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.575
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Course Foundations
and Scope as a question about inputs,

2
00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:09.890
decisions, and observable results.

3
00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:17.258
NARRATOR: Real-world datasets are often large
and can be stored in either main memory or a

4
00:00:17.258 --> 00:00:17.718
database.

5
00:00:17.718 --> 00:00:25.263
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Student
teams will load a 100,000-row customer

6
00:00:25.263 --> 00:00:30.921
transaction dataset into a relational database
for initial exploration.

7
00:00:30.921 --> 00:00:37.386
NARRATOR: On screen, display a split-screen
graphic showing a database schema on the left

8
00:00:37.386 --> 00:00:43.850
and an in-memory DataFrame on the right, with
arrows indicating data flow between storage

9
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.312
types.

10
00:00:44.312 --> 00:00:50.456
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

11
00:00:50.456 --> 00:00:51.874
change the conclusion.

12
00:00:51.874 --> 00:00:57.921
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

13
00:00:58.034 --> 00:01:04.368
NARRATOR: Now define Knowledge Discovery
Process precisely enough that another learner

14
00:01:04.368 --> 00:01:06.671
could test the definition.

15
00:01:06.671 --> 00:01:13.759
NARRATOR: The emphasis is on how to use data
mining to describe datasets rather than making

16
00:01:13.759 --> 00:01:14.202
predictions.

17
00:01:14.202 --> 00:01:20.327
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case:
Instructors will demonstrate how clustering

18
00:01:20.327 --> 00:01:26.452
customer purchase patterns reveals market
segments without forecasting future sales.

19
00:01:26.452 --> 00:01:34.289
NARRATOR: On screen, show a flowchart labeled
'Knowledge Discovery Process' with nodes for

20
00:01:34.289 --> 00:01:39.715
data collection, cleaning, analysis, and
description, explicitly excluding a

21
00:01:39.715 --> 00:01:40.921
'prediction' node.

22
00:01:40.921 --> 00:01:47.648
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

23
00:01:47.648 --> 00:01:49.890
evidence would distinguish them.

24
00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:56.249
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

25
00:01:56.354 --> 00:02:02.539
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Data Exploration and Cleaning

26
00:02:02.539 --> 00:02:05.116
operates in a concrete sequence.

27
00:02:05.116 --> 00:02:11.530
NARRATOR: Data quality analysis makes
missingness, duplicates, inconsistent

28
00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:17.944
representation, integrity constraints,
sampling, and update behavior explicit.

29
00:02:17.944 --> 00:02:25.710
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will run a missingness report on a survey

30
00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:32.920
dataset, flagging 15% null values in the
'income' column and removing 3% duplicate

31
00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:33.475
records.

32
00:02:33.475 --> 00:02:40.275
NARRATOR: On screen, render a heatmap of
missing values across columns, with a callout

33
00:02:40.275 --> 00:02:45.132
box highlighting the 'income' column and a
duplicate count badge.

34
00:02:45.132 --> 00:02:52.632
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

35
00:02:52.632 --> 00:02:58.350
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

36
00:02:58.466 --> 00:03:04.608
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Data Preparation Workflows and make the

37
00:03:04.608 --> 00:03:06.025
decision criteria explicit.

38
00:03:06.025 --> 00:03:12.608
NARRATOR: Data-analysis workflows separate
data preparation, exploratory analysis,

39
00:03:12.608 --> 00:03:18.369
training, validation, testing, model
selection, and evaluation.

40
00:03:18.369 --> 00:03:25.337
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
pipeline will be built where raw data

41
00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:30.697
undergoes imputation and normalization before
entering the exploratory analysis phase.

42
00:03:30.697 --> 00:03:37.414
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a horizontal
pipeline diagram with seven distinct stages,

43
00:03:37.414 --> 00:03:43.572
each labeled with a workflow phase and
connected by directional arrows.

44
00:03:43.572 --> 00:03:50.422
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

45
00:03:50.422 --> 00:03:52.134
move the goalposts.

46
00:03:52.134 --> 00:03:59.306
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

47
00:03:59.426 --> 00:04:05.778
NARRATOR: Apply Supervised and Unsupervised
Methods to a worked situation instead of

48
00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:08.954
treating it as an isolated definition.

49
00:04:08.954 --> 00:04:16.297
NARRATOR: Supervised methods learn mappings
from examples paired with target labels, while

50
00:04:16.297 --> 00:04:19.969
unsupervised methods identify structure
without targets.

51
00:04:19.969 --> 00:04:26.612
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will compare a decision tree trained on

52
00:04:26.612 --> 00:04:33.766
labeled customer churn data against a k-means
clustering run on the same unlabeled features.

53
00:04:33.766 --> 00:04:41.094
NARRATOR: On screen, display two side-by-side
scatter plots: one with labeled points and a

54
00:04:41.094 --> 00:04:46.329
decision boundary, the other with unlabeled
points and cluster centroids.

55
00:04:46.329 --> 00:04:53.110
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

56
00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:54.563
at each step.

57
00:04:54.563 --> 00:04:59.391
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

58
00:04:59.498 --> 00:05:04.584
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in
Preprocessing and Leakage Prevention,

59
00:05:04.584 --> 00:05:09.104
including what improves and what becomes more
difficult.

60
00:05:09.104 --> 00:05:15.573
NARRATOR: Preprocessing must be fitted only on
permitted training data to avoid leakage.

61
00:05:15.573 --> 00:05:21.417
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case:
Instructors will demonstrate scaling a feature

62
00:05:21.417 --> 00:05:28.323
using only the training set statistics, then
applying the same transformation to the

63
00:05:28.323 --> 00:05:29.385
validation set.

64
00:05:29.385 --> 00:05:35.921
NARRATOR: On screen, show a before-and-after
comparison of a feature distribution, with a

65
00:05:35.921 --> 00:05:42.457
red 'leakage' warning icon over a graph where
validation statistics were incorrectly used

66
00:05:42.457 --> 00:05:43.463
for scaling.

67
00:05:43.463 --> 00:05:49.924
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

68
00:05:49.924 --> 00:05:50.385
acceptable.

69
00:05:50.385 --> 00:05:57.776
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

70
00:05:57.890 --> 00:06:04.687
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Pipelines and Consistency with an edge case

71
00:06:04.687 --> 00:06:08.652
and identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

72
00:06:08.652 --> 00:06:15.858
NARRATOR: Pipelines combine transformations
and estimators so the same operations are

73
00:06:15.858 --> 00:06:17.168
applied consistently.

74
00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:24.374
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A single
pipeline object will chain standardization,

75
00:06:24.374 --> 00:06:30.980
imputation, and clustering, ensuring identical
operations run on every data split.

76
00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:38.203
NARRATOR: On screen, render a stacked block
diagram where each layer represents a

77
00:06:38.203 --> 00:06:43.760
transformation or estimator, with a
'consistent application' label spanning the

78
00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:44.871
entire stack.

79
00:06:44.871 --> 00:06:51.151
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

80
00:06:51.151 --> 00:06:51.543
holds.

81
00:06:51.543 --> 00:06:57.531
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

82
00:06:57.531 --> 00:06:58.386
core idea.

83
00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:05.566
NARRATOR: Use Cross-Validation and Sampling to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

84
00:07:05.566 --> 00:07:07.924
causes, and corrective action.

85
00:07:07.924 --> 00:07:14.658
NARRATOR: Cross-validation repeats training
and validation across defined splits.

86
00:07:14.658 --> 00:07:21.261
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will execute a 5-fold cross-validation

87
00:07:21.261 --> 00:07:28.464
routine, rotating the validation set through
each dataset partition and recording accuracy

88
00:07:28.464 --> 00:07:29.064
scores.

89
00:07:29.065 --> 00:07:36.042
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a circular
diagram with five segments, each labeled with

90
00:07:36.042 --> 00:07:42.481
a fold number, and arrows showing data
rotation between training and validation

91
00:07:42.481 --> 00:07:43.018
phases.

92
00:07:43.018 --> 00:07:48.890
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

93
00:07:48.890 --> 00:07:52.627
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

94
00:07:52.627 --> 00:07:58.489
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

95
00:07:58.489 --> 00:07:59.955
and the test.

96
00:08:00.074 --> 00:08:05.717
NARRATOR: Connect Algorithm Selection and
Evaluation to the earlier ideas and explain

97
00:08:05.717 --> 00:08:09.008
how the pieces work as one system.

98
00:08:09.008 --> 00:08:14.625
NARRATOR: Grouping, dimensionality reduction,
classification, regression, and clustering

99
00:08:14.625 --> 00:08:20.945
answer different questions and require
appropriate assumptions and metrics.

100
00:08:20.945 --> 00:08:27.645
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case:
Instructors will match a regression model to

101
00:08:27.645 --> 00:08:34.345
predicting continuous revenue and a clustering
algorithm to segment user behavior, explaining

102
00:08:34.345 --> 00:08:37.695
the distinct evaluation metrics for each.

103
00:08:37.695 --> 00:08:45.492
NARRATOR: On screen, display a decision tree
flowchart where each branch leads to an

104
00:08:45.492 --> 00:08:51.618
algorithm type, with corresponding metric
badges (e.g., RMSE, silhouette score) attached

105
00:08:51.618 --> 00:08:53.289
to each leaf.

106
00:08:53.289 --> 00:09:02.339
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

107
00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:04.602
meaning across representations.

108
00:09:04.602 --> 00:09:11.580
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

109
00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:12.117
evidence.

110
00:09:12.242 --> 00:09:17.736
NARRATOR: Finish with Practical Analysis
Checklist as a practical checklist that can

111
00:09:17.736 --> 00:09:19.567
guide a new analysis.

112
00:09:19.567 --> 00:09:27.961
NARRATOR: Evaluation must match the task and
the cost of errors, while distribution shift,

113
00:09:27.961 --> 00:09:33.957
missing data, overfitting, and unexamined
sampling choices can invalidate conclusions.

114
00:09:33.957 --> 00:09:40.433
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Students
will complete a checklist verifying data

115
00:09:40.433 --> 00:09:46.909
provenance, checking for distribution drift
between train and test sets, and documenting

116
00:09:46.909 --> 00:09:47.988
sampling methodology.

117
00:09:47.988 --> 00:09:54.743
NARRATOR: On screen, show a clean checklist
graphic with five checkboxes, each paired with

118
00:09:54.743 --> 00:10:00.051
a brief validation step, and a final 'valid
conclusions' stamp icon.

119
00:10:00.051 --> 00:10:07.643
NARRATOR: For each checklist item, record the
evidence, the remaining uncertainty, and the

120
00:10:07.643 --> 00:10:09.395
next verification step.

121
00:10:09.395 --> 00:10:15.221
NARRATOR: The result is a repeatable
procedure, not a conclusion that depends on

122
00:10:15.221 --> 00:10:16.566
memory or intuition.
